  "query_preview": "(This evening through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 312 PM EDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nWarm fall weather will be the rule through Thursday as upper ridging \nbuilds back over the SE CONUS and 850 mb temps rise to around 17 C. \nHigh temps will be in the 80s most areas Wednesday and areawide \nThursday. A weak upper level disturbance located over OK/KS will \nrotate around the upper ridge and graze central Indiana during \nWednesday. As a result sky cover has been increased to mostly cloudy \nfor areas north of I-70. Steep mid level lapse rates will support \nelevated showers, but low levels will remain very dry, so only \nscattered sprinkles are expected from a mid level cloud ceiling. \nWinds will gradually increase on Wednesday as the pressure gradient \nsteadily increases between surface high pressure over the srn Apps \nand low pressure over the central Plains. By Thursday, this gradient \nwill increase sufficiently to support breezy conditions with south \ngusts near 20 mph during the afternoon. \n\nAttention turns to the main forecast challenge/event during the \nperiod which is timing of arrival, duration and amounts of rainfall \nlate Thursday into the weekend. Most model guidance suggests a \nfairly short period of strongest forcing/heaviest rainfall during \nthe 06Z-18Z period Friday as a frontal boundary shifts southward \nthrough central Indiana. This shorter temporal rain threat than \nearlier forecasts advertised is due to several factors. The primary \nfactors are the faster weakening of the mass fields with the remnant \nshortwave over the plains and a resultant increasing tendency for a \nstronger area of high pressure over the upper MS valley. The net \nresult is stronger pressure rises supporting a greater southward \ndisplacement of the main baroclinic zone. As far as rainfall \namounts, PWATs are still expected to be very high for early October, \naround 1.5 inches. Therefore, even with the shorter duration of \nrainfall, most areas will see between 0.75 to 1.25 inch QPF during \nthis period. Even with a shorter duration of rainfall, these amounts \nare low enough that no hydro concerns are expected. \n\nIn the wake of this rainfall, there is still a few outlier models \n(AI based) showing a weak tropical surge attempting to make it up to \nIndiana Friday night into early Sunday. Low pops were kept for the \noutside chance of showers during this period across southern \nportions of central Indiana in closer proximity to the \nforcing/frontal boundary. The aforementioned reinforcing area of \nhigh pressure combined with stronger CAA (850 temps fall around 8 C \nfrom 18Z Sunday to 06Z Monday) should shift any threat for \nadditional precip well south of the area. \n\nThe coldest night of the fall is expected Monday night as a 1030mb \nhigh pressure builds southward through the Great Lakes. Lows in the \nlow-mid 40s are expected areawide with a few outlying locations \npossibly falling into upper 30s Tuesday morning. There will also \nlikely be some areas of fog during this period with light winds and \nrecent rainfall. \n\nLooking further into the extended, expecting broad NW flow to \nmaintain a fairly typical dry and benign fall pattern through much \nof next week into the following weekend with temps expected to be \nnear or slightly below normal with highs in the mid 60s-mid 70s and \nlows in the 40s to lower 50s.",
